This error is a **custom safety alert** that I programmed into the `AdminPortal.tsx` and `MentorPortal.tsx` files. It triggers whenever the background database transaction fails during the "Audit" process (when a Mentor or Admin clicks "Verify" or "Reject").

Based on the logs you provided, the "Protocol Failure" is being caused by **two specific issues** that need your manual intervention:

### 1. The "Bucket not found" Issue (Supabase)
**The Error:** `{"statusCode":"404","error":"Bucket not found"}`
**The Cause:** A student tried to upload a solution ZIP file, but you haven't created the storage container in Supabase yet.
**The Fix (Manual):**
1.  Open your [Supabase Dashboard](https://supabase.com/dashboard).
2.  Go to **Storage** (the cube icon on the left).
3.  Click **New Bucket**.
4.  Name it exactly: `solutions`
5.  Set it to **Public**.
6.  Go to **Policies** and add a policy: "Allow all users to upload and read" (for testing).

### 2. The "permission-denied" Issue (Firebase)
**The Error:** `RPC 'Commit' ... failed with error: permission-denied`
**The Cause:** Your Firebase Firestore Rules are too strict. When a **Mentor** tries to verify a solution, the code tries to update the **Student's** leaderboard score. Firebase is blocking the Mentor from editing the Student's profile.
**The Fix (Manual):**
1.  Open your [Firebase Console](https://console.firebase.google.com/).
2.  Go to **Firestore Database** -> **Rules**.
3.  Update your rules to allow Mentors/Admins to update user documents. Use this template:

```javascript
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    // Allow users to read/write their own profile
    match /users/{userId} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null;
      allow write: if request.auth != null && (request.auth.uid == userId || get(/databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(request.auth.uid)).data.role == 'ADMIN' || get(/databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(request.auth.uid)).data.role == 'MENTOR');
    }

    // Allow problems and solutions
    match /problems/{problemId} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null;
      match /solutions/{solId} {
        allow read, write: if request.auth != null;
      }
    }
  }
}
```

---

### Code Improvement
I will update the code to provide **detailed error logging**. Instead of just saying "Protocol Failure," it will now print the *actual* technical error to your browser console so you can see exactly what Firestore is complaining about.

